<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeddah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jidda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeddah is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Made the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Uthman in the 7th century, it became the main gateway for pilgrims arriving by sea and a centre of Red Sea trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> took it in 1925, and it served as Saudi Arabia's diplomatic capital and remains its chief commercial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeddah.jpg" alt="Jeddah" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeddah_Waterfront,_Western_Saudi_Arabia_(2026).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jeddah lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coastal plain, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihamah</a>, in the lower Hijaz mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, the holiest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, is 65 kilometres to the east, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, the second holiest, is 360 kilometres to the north. The climate is arid, and unlike other Saudi cities Jeddah keeps warm temperatures in winter, while summers often pass 40 °C. Traces of early activity are attested by Thamudic inscriptions found in Wadi Briman and Wadi Boweb, and the oldest mashrabiya, a type of carved wooden screen, found in Jeddah dates to the pre-Islamic era. One account of the name derives it from the Arabic word jaddah, meaning grandmother, and a folk belief places the tomb of Eve, the grandmother of humanity, in the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jeddah first achieved prominence around 647 CE, when the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthman-ibn-affan">Uthman ibn Affan</a>, made it the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in place of the Al Shoaib port, southwest of Mecca. In 969 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, who had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from the Abbasid governors of the Ikhshidid dynasty, extended their empire to the Hijaz and Jeddah, and developed trade networks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, with diplomatic ties reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In 1517 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>, conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and in 1802 forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> took Mecca and Jeddah from them. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> ordered his Egyptian viceroy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, to retake the city, and he did so in the Battle of Jeddah in 1813.</p><p>During the First World War Sharif Hussein bin Ali declared a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, seeking a unified Arab state from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. Ibn Saud, whose clan came from the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a>, later conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and Jeddah by agreement with the people of Jeddah after the Second Battle of Jeddah. He deposed Ali bin Hussein, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and eventually settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>, where his descendants joined the Hashemite royal house of Jordan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jeddah is the second-largest city in Saudi Arabia after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> and its main commercial centre, and the principal gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. It has long been a port: in the 19th century it exported mother-of-pearl, tortoise shells, frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. The old city, Al-Balad, with its multistorey merchant houses, was granted UNESCO World Heritage status in 2014, and it holds the Bayt Naseef museum. The speech of the region is called the Hejazi dialect. The 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,712,917 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeddah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The population reached 32 million in 2022, about 85% of them urban in Riyadh, <b>Jeddah</b> and Dammam, half under 25 and close to 42% immigrants; about 80% of private-sector employees are foreign, many under the kafala system that rights groups link to slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He hoped to conquer the Ethiopian highlands, and the province's seat moved between Massawa, Suakin and later <b>Jeddah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom controlled the Hejaz, its holy cities, the pilgrimage and the port of <b>Jeddah</b>, financed largely by British subsidies and pilgrim fees.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Kingdom of Hejaz</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sharifs kept order in Mecca and the pilgrimage roads, collected dues from pilgrims and <b>Jeddah's</b> port and balanced Bedouin tribes.</blockquote><a href="/state/sharifate-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a></li><li><blockquote>After Mecca and then <b>Jeddah</b> fell, Ibn Saud was proclaimed King of the Hejaz in January 1926, joining his two realms as the Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-nejd">Sultanate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
